          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I ate shellfish ten minutes ago and now my lips are swollen and my throat feels tight. Should I use my adrenaline pen?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** The safety check flagged this as an emergency. Its guidance says: \"Call emergency services now. If an adrenaline auto-injector is available, use it immediately into the outer thigh, per the device's own instructions, before or while calling.\" (Source: the risk check, which matched the anaphylaxis guidance.)\n\nSwollen lips, a tight throat, and symptoms starting minutes after eating a known trigger can be signs of anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction. The anaphylaxis guidance lists shellfish as a common trigger. It also says a reaction can get worse, so anyone who has had one needs medical observation even if it improves. (Source: \"Anaphylaxis and severe allergic reaction,\" red-flag-anaphylaxis.)\n\nIf you have your own adrenaline pen and you have been prescribed it, follow the instructions on the device and your action plan. Do not wait to see if it gets better. Ask someone nearby to call emergency services while you do this, and tell them when the symptoms started. If you have trouble breathing, the breathing guidance says adrenaline comes first when anaphylaxis is possible. (Source: \"Severe difficulty breathing,\" red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty.)\n\nI can't tell you whether to use your pen. The reference material I have does not give dosing or personal instructions, and only a clinician or emergency responder can assess you. Please get help now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
